The approach is Kahn's algorithm with a min-heap. Every node mentioned anywhere, whether as a key or only as a dependency, goes into the graph. Each node's in-degree is the count of its dependencies. Nodes with in-degree zero go into a heap ordered by name, so the alphabetically smallest ready node is always popped first. When a node is output, its dependents lose one in-degree and any that reach zero enter the heap. If the output has fewer nodes than the graph, a cycle exists and ValueError is raised. Each edge is processed once, so this runs in O((V+E) log V).

